<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 8: Handling Missing Values                               -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Opens with a real, verified story: Abraham Wald and the           -->
<!--  Statistical Research Group's WWII aircraft-survivability work.    -->
<!--  Chosen deliberately over a "disaster" story (unlike ch2/ch4):     -->
<!--  it is the classic case of reasoning correctly, and it is exactly  -->
<!--  about the imputation/deletion-adjacent question this chapter      -->
<!--  needs (why is data missing, and what does that imply about how    -->
<!--  to treat it), which none of the ch4 stories (inconsistency,       -->
<!--  duplication, silent recoding) actually covered.                  -->
<!--  Verified via multiple independent searches before writing:       -->
<!--    - Wald, A. (1943). "A Method of Estimating Plane Vulnerability  -->
<!--      Based on Damage of Survivors." Statistical Research Group,    -->
<!--      Columbia University, memorandum for the National Defense     -->
<!--      Research Committee (reprinted, Center for Naval Analyses,    -->
<!--      1980).                                                       -->
<!--    - Mangel, M. and Samaniego, F.J. (1984). "Abraham Wald's Work   -->
<!--      on Aircraft Survivability." Journal of the American           -->
<!--      Statistical Association, 79(386), 259-267. Peer-reviewed      -->
<!--      formalisation of Wald's original (classified at the time)     -->
<!--      memoranda - used as the authoritative secondary source for    -->
<!--      what his method actually did technically, specifically to    -->
<!--      avoid the oversimplified pop-science "just armour the         -->
<!--      engines" version. His actual method: assume hits are roughly  -->
<!--      uniformly distributed over the airframe by surface area, then -->
<!--      use returning-plane damage counts to infer conditional        -->
<!--      survival/vulnerability by section, explicitly correcting for  -->
<!--      the missing (non-returning) planes rather than reading        -->
<!--      returning-plane damage at face value.                        -->
<!--  MCAR/MAR/MNAR formal definitions and the na.rm/complete.cases     -->
<!--  material are adapted from the old, unbuilt drafts                -->
<!--  (ch6_descriptive_statistics.ptx sec-missing-data, and             -->
<!--  ch6_description_2.ptx subsec-missing-formal), rewritten to open   -->
<!--  with Wald as the MNAR example rather than presenting the taxonomy -->
<!--  cold.                                                             -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-handling-missing-values" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Handling Missing Values</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-handling-missing-values">
    <p>
      In <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>, we ran into <c>NA</c> as soon as we
      tried to calculate a mean, and set the problem aside.
      In <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, we looked at the gaps, and started
      to think informally about why VARIBABLES specifically had NAs.
    </p>

    <p>
      The big idea in this chapter is easy to say, and easy to underestimate:
      <em>why</em> a value is missing decides what you should do about it.
      Sometimes missing values are just background noise, and you can safely
      ignore them.
      Sometimes they're the most important information in the whole dataset.
      The study below is one where working out that difference really mattered.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: Wald and the bombers                                  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-intro-missing-values">
    <title>The Patients Who Aren't in the Data</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, the problem was ...
        .
        This case study is about [..]: data that should have existed, but simply
        isn't there at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        Adhiambo et al.
        (2025) followed adults getting HIV treatment at five health facilities
        in western Kenya, and asked a question that sounds simple: how many of
        them had missed a routine blood test they were supposed to have?
        The answer turned out to depend completely on working out why each
        person was missing, and not just how many were.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-background">
      <title>A test that tells you if treatment is working</title>

      <p>
        People living with HIV take antiretroviral therapy (ART) to keep the
        virus suppressed, ideally to levels so low that it can't be transmitted
        and can't seriously damage the body.
        The most direct way to know whether that's actually happening for a
        given patient is a <term>viral load</term> test: a blood test that
        measures how much virus is circulating.
        <fn>
    It isn't the only way. Before viral load testing became routine, clinics
    relied on CD4 cell counts (a proxy that tracks immune system damage) and
    on watching for clinical symptoms of disease progression. Both work, but
    both lag behind. Viral load can rise for months before CD4 count falls or
    symptoms appear, so a patient whose treatment is failing may go
    undetected for a long time under either older method. That's why viral
    load testing was treated as such a major improvement when it was
    introduced.
  </fn>
        Kenyan national guidelines, in line with World Health Organization
        recommendations, say this test should happen 6 and 12 months after
        starting treatment, and every 12 months after that.
      </p>

      <p>
        The first year is when treatment failure is most likely, and a timely
        viral load result is what lets a clinician catch it early, before drug
        resistance develops or the patient's health declines further.
        A missed viral load test could mean a patient has failing treatment,
        with nobody currently able to tell.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-study">
      <title>Following 1,754 people for two years</title>

      <p>
        The study sat inside a larger clinical trial running from 2014 to 2019,
        across three urban clinics in Kisumu County and two more rural clinics
        in Migori County.
        Viral load monitoring wasn't managed specially for the trial.
        It happened through ordinary, routine clinic care, which is exactly what
        makes the results useful: they show what actually happens in the health
        system, not what happens under ideal research conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        For every one of 1,754 participants, the researchers checked whether a
        viral load result existed in the 9 to 15 month window after starting
        treatment, and again in the 21 to 27 month window.
        If a result couldn't be found in the routine records, that wasn't the
        end of the process.
        The researchers went further: checking laboratory logs, a national
        database, paper files, and eventually contacting clinicians directly, to
        work out, case by case, what had actually happened to that missing test.
        Later in this chapter, we'll come back to why that second step mattered
        so much.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-headline-number">
      <title>The number you'd report first</title>

      <p>
        In year one, 428 of 1,754 participants, 24.4%, had no viral load result
        in their window.
        In year two, it was 502 of 1,709, 29.4%.
        Almost a third of patients, on the most basic reading of the data,
        simply didn't have the information their doctors needed.
      </p>

      <p>
        Faced with a gap like this, the easiest thing to do is to just work with
        the data you have.
        Take the roughly 1,300 people who do have a year-one viral load result,
        calculate whatever you need from them, (look at `urban vs rural`, `older
        vs younger`), and assume it applies to everyone.
      </p>

      <p>
        Faced with a gap like this, the easiest thing to do is to just work with
        what you have.
        Take the roughly 1,300 people who do have a year-one viral load result,
        calculate whatever you need from them, urban vs rural, older vs younger,
        and assume it applies to everyone.
      </p>

      <p>
        Think about a class of 100 students sitting an exam, where 24 are absent
        on the day.
        If you calculate the class average from the 76 who showed up, is that a
        fair estimate of how the whole class of 100 would have done?
        It depends entirely on <em>why</em> those 24 weren't there.
        If they were kept home by 24 unrelated colds and stomach bugs, there's
        no reason to think they'd have scored any differently, so the average
        from the 76 is a perfectly good stand-in for all 100.
        But if most of the 24 stayed home because they hadn't done the reading
        and didn't want to sit the exam, the 76 who showed up are, on average,
        the more prepared half of the class.
        Their average isn't a fair estimate of everyone anymore.
        It's an estimate of the students who choose to turn up when they're
        ready.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same question applies to the 428 people missing a year-one viral
        load.
        Working with just the 1,300 who were tested is only safe if <em>why</em>
        someone went untested has nothing to do with whether their treatment was
        working.
        In this dataset, it turns out that isn't the case.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-why">
      <title>Ah, but why is it missing?</title>

      <p>
        The extended investigation gave the missing results a reason, and the
        reasons were not evenly spread.
        In year one, of the 406 participants who were still alive and missing a
        test, 149 (36.7%) were missing because a clinician misread the
        monitoring schedule, ordering the next test 12 months after the
        <em>last</em> viral load rather than 12 months after treatment started.
        209 (51.5%) had missed every clinic appointment during the testing
        window and couldn't be found at all.
        42 (10.3%) had no documented reason.
        And 6 (1.5%) had a test ordered by a clinician, with no record that a
        sample was ever collected.
      </p>

      <p>
        Four reasons, and as we'll see, each one changes what the missing data
        is quietly telling you.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-mcar">
      <title>Some people are missing for no reason at all</title>

      <p>
        There are 6 participants in year one, and 6 again in year two, whose
        clinician ordered a viral load test, and where a sample was, apparently,
        simply never collected.
        No record of a patient refusal.
        No record of a clinical decision.
        Just a test that should have happened, and didn't, for reasons that look
        like nothing more than an administrative slip.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the closest thing in the dataset to missingness that has nothing
        to do with the patient at all.
        It's the "kept home by an unrelated cold" case from our exam: whatever
        happened to these six people, it wasn't connected to whether their
        treatment was working.
        Losing these six records doesn't quietly shift your results in any
        particular direction.
        There's no reason to think they'd have looked any different from the
        patients who were tested, so leaving them out costs you six data points,
        and nothing else.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-mar">
      <title>Some people are missing because of who they are</title>

      <p>
        Two other characteristics also predicted missingness, but in a different
        way.
        Participants under 24 had more than double the risk of a missed viral
        load compared to those over 44, and participants at rural clinics had a
        higher risk than those at urban clinics.
        Neither of these is a measurement of how sick someone is.
        They're just facts about the patient that happen to be recorded already,
        in every participant's file, tested or not.
      </p>

      <p>
        That difference matters.
        Can we assume that younger and older patients would show the same viral
        suppression results, if we could see everyone's?
        Not necessarily; the paper suggests younger patients are more mobile,
        and more likely to fall out of care for reasons that have nothing to do
        with how their treatment is going.
        Can we assume rural and urban patients would look the same?
        Again, probably not exactly, since rural clinics face harder equipment
        access and longer sample transport.
        But because age and clinic location are known for every single
        participant, missing or not, they at least give you something to work
        with.
        You can compare rural patients to rural patients, and under-24s to
        under-24s, instead of guessing at the group as a whole.
        A gap you can explain using information you already have!
        But, assuming these were
      </p>

      <p>
        That difference matters.
        Unlike the six lost samples, we can't just assume these absences were
        unrelated to anything, the way we could with an unlucky cold.
        Think back to the exam: imagine the 24 absent students weren't a random
        slice of the class, but were disproportionately the students who come to
        school from a further, poorer part of town, and they didn't make it in
        that day.
        Averaging just the students who made it in would quietly overrepresent
        those who live closer, and you'd have no way to know it, unless you'd
        also recorded where each student lived.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's the difference here.
        Age and clinic location aren't a guess.
        They're recorded for every single participant, tested or not.
        So instead of assuming the untested under-24s would have looked like the
        tested over-44s, or that untested rural patients would have looked like
        tested urban ones, we can do something more careful: compare like with
        like.
        Look at suppression rates among tested under-24s, and treat that as your
        best estimate for untested under-24s, rather than borrowing an estimate
        from a different age group entirely.
        A gap you can explain using information you already have is a very
        different problem from a gap you can only explain using the very thing
        you're trying to measure, which is exactly the case we turn to next.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-mnar">
      <title>And some people are missing because they're sicker</title>

      <p>
        The study's analysis found that participants at the most advanced stage
        of HIV disease (WHO clinical stage 4), had more than twice the risk of
        missing their year-two viral load compared to those at stage 1.
        The research team also learned something that never made it into any
        official record at all: some clinicians told them, when asked directly,
        that they had deliberately avoided sending viral load tests for patients
        they suspected were already failing treatment, because they already
        knew, or believed, the result would come back high.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the most dangerous kind of missing data there is, because the
        reason for the gap is tangled up with the very thing you're trying to
        measure.
        Go back to the exam one last time.
        This is the student who stayed home because they hadn't done the reading
        and didn't want to sit a test they knew they'd fail.
        Their absence isn't unrelated to their score, like the cold, and it
        isn't something you could correct for with a note about which bus they
        take, like the traffic.
        Their absence <em>is</em> their score, in the sense that not showing up
        is itself evidence of how unprepared they were.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same is true here.
        The patients missing from the "viral load" column aren't a random slice
        of everyone, and they aren't a slice you can identify from something
        else in their file, the way you could identify an under-24 or a rural
        patient.
        They're disproportionately the patients doing worst, and the very fact
        of their disease progressing is bound up with why they weren't tested.
        If you calculate what fraction of tested patients are virally suppressed
        and treat that as the answer for everyone, you'll come out too
        optimistic, because the sickest people were the ones most likely never
        to be tested in the first place.
        There's no group to compare like with like here, the way there was for
        age or clinic location.
        The information you'd need to correct for the gap is the same
        information that's missing.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-lesson">
      <title>Missingness is not one problem</title>

      <p>
        Put these different reasons side by side, and you can see why treating
        "24.4% missing" as a single fact would have been a mistake.
        Lost to follow-up, which accounts for over half of all missing cases,
        could mean a death that was never recorded, a self-transfer to another
        clinic, or a patient who is very much alive and simply out of care.
        Clinician misinterpretation of the guidelines, more than a third,
        reflects a scheduling error, unrelated to the patient's health at all.
        Advanced disease stage reflects the sickest patients disappearing from
        the very data used to judge how well treatment is working.
        And the handful of lost samples reflect nothing more than bad luck.
      </p>

      <p>
        Adhiambo et al.
        (2025) are explicit that this changes what the topline numbers can be
        trusted to say.
        Missed viral load monitoring, they write,
        <q>remains high, primarily due to loss to follow-up, and may
        substantially alter suppression estimates.</q>
        Once the team understood that the missing data wasn't random, they
        stopped relying on the case database for routine reporting and used
        laboratory data for day-to-day reports instead.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's the habit this chapter is trying to build.
        A missing value is never just an empty cell.
        Before you decide whether to drop it, fill it in, or flag it and move
        on, you have to ask why it's empty.
        Sometimes that question has a simple answer, and you can safely ignore
        the gap.
        Sometimes it has an answer tied to information you already have
        elsewhere, and you can work around it.
        And sometimes, as with the sickest patients in this study, the answer is
        tied to the exact thing you were trying to measure, and ignoring it
        could seriously bias your study.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Adhiambo, H.F., Lewis-Kulzer, J., Nyagesoa, E.,
        Gimbel, S., Akama, E., Mangale, D., Montonya, L., Eshun-Wilson, I.,
        Iguna, S., Nyandieka, E., Bukusi, E., Abuogi, L., Odeny, T., Petersen,
        M.L.
        and Geng, E.H.
        (2025).
        <q>Examining and classifying reasons for missing viral loads among
        adults living with HIV: An extended outcome investigation and
        ascertainment approach in Western Kenya.</q>
        <em>PLOS Global Public Health</em>, 5(5), e0004038.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch8-stack">
    <title>STACK question</title>

    <p>
      TODO: add STACK question
    </p>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: applied to the africa_indicators data                 -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-missing-data-r">
    <title>Missing Data in Practice: African Development Indicators</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The viral load study had something most of us never get: a team who went
        and found out why each result was missing.
        In practice, we usually have the dataset and nothing else, so we have to
        work out what we can from the data itself.
        This section does that, using the <c>africa_indicators</c> dataset.
      </p>

      <p>
        It holds World Bank development indicators for the 54 African countries,
        one row per country per year from 2010 to 2023.
        There are 756 rows, with population, GDP per capita, life expectancy,
        under-5 mortality, HIV prevalence, births attended by skilled health
        staff, primary school enrolment and the poverty rate.
        We chose it because the missing values are real, they are everywhere,
        and (as we'll see) they are missing for very different reasons.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        library(statsdata)
        library(dplyr)
        library(ggplot2)
        library(naniar)

        data(africa_indicators)
        </code>
      </program>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-missing-finding">
      <title>Finding missing values</title>

      <p>
        The first step is to find out where the missing values are, and how many
        there are.
        In R, <c>is.na()</c> returns <c>TRUE</c> for each missing value and
        <c>FALSE</c> for each value that is there.
        Wrapping it in <c>sum()</c> counts the missing values in a column:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # Is births_skilled missing for the first six rows (Algeria, 2010-2015)?
        head(is.na(africa_indicators$births_skilled))
        #&gt; [1]  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE FALSE  TRUE  TRUE

        # How many missing values in primary_enrolment?
        sum(is.na(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment))
        #&gt; [1] 219
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        To check every column at once, <c>colSums(is.na())</c> does the same
        thing across the whole dataset:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        colSums(is.na(africa_indicators))
        #&gt;              iso3           country            region      income_group
        #&gt;                 0                 0                 0                 0
        #&gt;              year        population    gdp_per_capita   life_expectancy
        #&gt;                 0                 0                20                 0
        #&gt;  under5_mortality    hiv_prevalence    births_skilled primary_enrolment
        #&gt;                 0                56               583               219
        #&gt;      poverty_rate
        #&gt;               634
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Five of the thirteen columns have missing values, and they are nothing
        like each other.
        <c>gdp_per_capita</c> has 20 missing values out of 756 (under 3%), while
        <c>poverty_rate</c> has 634 (84%).
      </p>

      <p>
        The <c>naniar</c> package gives us some visual tools.
        <c>vis_miss()</c> draws a heatmap of the whole dataset, with missing
        values in a dark colour:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        vis_miss(africa_indicators)
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-africa-vis-miss">
        <caption>
          Missingness heatmap for the <c>africa_indicators</c> dataset.
          Each row is one country in one year, and each column is one variable.
          Missing values are dark.
          <c>births_skilled</c> and <c>poverty_rate</c> are mostly missing,
          <c>primary_enrolment</c> is patchy, and <c>gdp_per_capita</c> and
          <c>hiv_prevalence</c> have a few solid blocks.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch-missing-vis-miss.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A heatmap with one row per country-year and one column per
              variable.
              Most columns are entirely light grey.
              The births_skilled and poverty_rate columns are mostly dark,
              primary_enrolment has scattered dark stripes, and gdp_per_capita
              and hiv_prevalence each have a few short dark blocks.
              The percentage missing is printed in each column label.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Look at the blocks in <c>gdp_per_capita</c> and <c>hiv_prevalence</c>.
        A block is a run of consecutive rows, and since the rows are sorted by
        country, a block means one country that has no value in any year.
        That isn't what scattered, unlucky gaps look like!
      </p>

      <p>
        <c>gg_miss_var()</c> gives a bar chart of the number of missing values
        per variable, which is easier to read when there are lots of columns:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        gg_miss_var(africa_indicators)
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-africa-gg-miss-var">
        <caption>
          Number of missing values per variable in the <c>africa_indicators</c>
          dataset.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch-missing-gg-miss-var.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A horizontal bar chart with variable names on the vertical axis
              and the number of missing values on the horizontal axis.
              poverty_rate is longest at 634, then births_skilled at 583,
              primary_enrolment at 219, hiv_prevalence at 56 and gdp_per_capita
              at 20.
              All other variables have none.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-missing-patterns">
      <title>Patterns of missingness</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          We now know how many values are missing from each variable.
          But are those missing values scattered throughout the data, or do they
          follow a pattern?
        </p>

        <p>
          Think back to the <c>vis_miss</c> graph above.
          Some variables had long blocks of missing values, while others had
          gaps scattered across different rows.
          These patterns might tell us something about why the values are
          missing.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let's investigate a few of the variables to see what we can find out.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Are entire countries missing?</title>

        <p>
          Start with <c>hiv_prevalence</c>.
          The graph showed a large block of missing values.
          Are these spread across many countries, or concentrated in just a few?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  filter(is.na(hiv_prevalence)) %&gt;%
  count(country, income_group)

# A tibble: 4 x 3
  country               income_group            n
  &lt;chr&gt;                 &lt;fct&gt;               &lt;int&gt;
1 Equatorial Guinea     Upper middle income     14
2 Mauritius             Upper middle income     14
3 Sao Tome and Principe Lower middle income     14
4 Seychelles            High income             14
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          All 56 missing values come from just four countries.
          For each of these countries, HIV prevalence is missing for all 14
          years.
          This is quite different from having a few missing observations
          scattered across the dataset.
        </p>

        <p>
          Does it matter which countries are missing?
          Let's compare these four countries with the countries that do have HIV
          prevalence data.
          We cannot compare their HIV prevalence, since that is precisely what
          is missing, but we can compare other variables.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(hiv_missing = is.na(hiv_prevalence)) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    mean_under5 = mean(under5_mortality),
    mean_gdp = mean(gdp_per_capita, na.rm = TRUE),
    n = n()
  )

# A tibble: 2 x 4
  hiv_missing mean_under5 mean_gdp     n
  &lt;lgl&gt;             &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
1 FALSE              69.7    2145.   700
2 TRUE               35.1    9430.    56
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          There is quite a difference!
          The four countries without HIV prevalence data have, on average, about
          half the under-5 mortality and more than four times the GDP per capita
          of the countries with data.
          These are averages across country-years, and there are differences
          between the four countries themselves.
        </p>

        <p>
          Why does this matter?
          Suppose we wanted to investigate the relationship between HIV
          prevalence and economic development across Africa.
          We would have no HIV prevalence data for these four countries, so they
          could not be included in that analysis.
          Our results would describe the countries with data, which are not
          necessarily representative of all African countries.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Are particular years missing?</title>

        <p>
          Next, consider <c>gdp_per_capita</c>.
          Which countries have missing values?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  filter(is.na(gdp_per_capita)) %&gt;%
  count(country)

# A tibble: 2 x 2
  country         n
  &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;int&gt;
1 Eritrea        12
2 South Sudan     8
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Only two countries have missing GDP per capita values.
          But unlike HIV prevalence, where every year was missing for four
          countries, these countries do have some GDP data.
          Which years are missing?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  filter(is.na(gdp_per_capita)) %&gt;%
  group_by(country) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    first_missing = min(year),
    last_missing = max(year),
    n_missing = n()
  )

# A tibble: 2 x 4
  country     first_missing last_missing n_missing
  &lt;chr&gt;               &lt;int&gt;        &lt;int&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1 Eritrea              2012         2023        12
2 South Sudan          2016         2023         8
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Eritrea has no GDP per capita figures from 2012 onwards, while South
          Sudan has none from 2016 onwards.
          In both cases, the missing values form one long, uninterrupted period.
        </p>

        <p>
          Why might this happen?
          GDP figures depend on national statistical systems collecting and
          compiling economic information.
          Political instability, conflict, difficulties collecting data, or
          delays in producing national accounts can all affect the availability
          of these figures.
        </p>

        <p>
          Both Eritrea and South Sudan have faced substantial political and
          economic challenges during these periods.
          However, we cannot tell from this dataset alone exactly why their GDP
          figures are missing.
          We would need to investigate the original data source and its
          documentation.
        </p>

        <p>
          What we can say is that the gaps are not spread evenly across
          countries or years.
          If we removed every row without GDP per capita, we would lose most of
          Eritrea's observations and all of South Sudan's observations from 2016
          onwards.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Does missingness relate to other variables?</title>

        <p>
          Let's turn to <c>primary_enrolment</c>.
          We have seen that missing values can be concentrated in particular
          countries and years.
          Could they also be associated with other characteristics?
        </p>

        <p>
          First, let's look at the proportion of missing values within each
          income group.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(income_group) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    n_total = n(),
    n_missing = sum(is.na(primary_enrolment)),
    pct_missing = round(n_missing / n_total * 100, 1)
  )

# A tibble: 4 x 4
  income_group        n_total n_missing pct_missing
  &lt;fct&gt;                 &lt;int&gt;     &lt;int&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Low income              294        92        31.3
2 Lower middle income     336        81        24.1
3 Upper middle income     112        46        41.1
4 High income              14         0         0
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The proportion missing varies considerably.
          Around 41% of upper-middle-income observations are missing, compared
          with 24% of lower-middle-income observations.
          The high-income group has no missing values, although this group
          contains only Seychelles, so we should be careful about drawing
          conclusions from it.
        </p>

        <p>
          What about time?
          Are some years more likely to have missing enrolment data than others?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(year) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    pct_missing = mean(is.na(primary_enrolment)) * 100
  ) %&gt;%
  ggplot(aes(x = year, y = pct_missing)) +
  geom_col(fill = "#F37021") +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = 2010:2023) +
  labs(
    x = "Year",
    y = "Countries with no value (%)"
  ) +
  theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-africa-enrolment-year">
          <caption>
            The percentage of countries with missing primary enrolment figures,
            by year.
            Missingness increases in the later years of the dataset.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch-missing-enrolment-year.png" width="90%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A bar chart showing the percentage of countries missing primary
                enrolment data each year from 2010 to 2023.
                The percentage increases after 2015, reaching approximately 40%
                in several years from 2019 onwards.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Missing values become much more common in recent years.
          This could partly reflect delays in collecting, processing, or
          publishing education statistics.
          Again, the graph shows us the pattern, but not necessarily its cause.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is one more question worth asking.
          Are the country-years with missing enrolment data different from those
          with available data?
        </p>

        <p>
          We cannot compare their enrolment rates, because those are missing.
          But we can compare under-5 mortality and life expectancy.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(
    enrolment_missing = is.na(primary_enrolment)
  ) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    mean_under5 = mean(under5_mortality),
    mean_life = mean(life_expectancy),
    n = n()
  )

# A tibble: 2 x 4
  enrolment_missing mean_under5 mean_life     n
  &lt;lgl&gt;                   &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
1 FALSE                    63.7      62.9   537
2 TRUE                     75.6      61.6   219
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Country-years without enrolment data have higher under-5 mortality:
          75.6 deaths per 1,000 live births, compared with 63.7 where enrolment
          data is available.
          They also have slightly lower life expectancy.
        </p>

        <p>
          This does not mean that missing enrolment data causes higher child
          mortality, or that we know the missing enrolment rates are lower.
          But it does show that the observations with missing data differ in
          other important ways.
        </p>

        <p>
          Imagine that we wanted to study the relationship between primary
          school enrolment and child health.
          If we simply removed all the rows with missing enrolment, we would
          disproportionately exclude observations with higher child mortality.
          That could affect the conclusions we draw.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>What if the data was never collected?</title>

        <p>
          Finally, let's look at <c>poverty_rate</c> and <c>births_skilled</c>.
          Both have many missing values, but there may be a different
          explanation for these gaps.
        </p>

        <p>
          Consider Kenya's data.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  filter(country == "Kenya") %&gt;%
  select(year, births_skilled, poverty_rate)

# A tibble: 14 x 3
    year births_skilled poverty_rate
   &lt;int&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;
 1  2010           NA           NA
 2  2011           NA           NA
 3  2012           NA           NA
 4  2013           NA           NA
 5  2014           61.8         NA
 6  2015           NA           37.7
 7  2016           70.2         NA
 8  2017           NA           NA
 9  2018           NA           NA
10  2019           NA           NA
11  2020           NA           44.4
12  2021           NA           46.4
13  2022           NA           45.5
14  2023           NA           NA
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Kenya has just two values for births attended by skilled health
          personnel, and four for poverty, across 14 years.
        </p>

        <p>
          Why might this be?
          Unlike some indicators that are estimated annually, these measures
          often rely on household surveys.
          Large surveys are expensive and time-consuming, so they are generally
          conducted periodically rather than every year.
        </p>

        <p>
          For example, Kenya's Demographic and Health Surveys collect
          information about maternal and child health, including assistance
          during childbirth.
          Poverty estimates also depend heavily on household survey information.
        </p>

        <p>
          A missing value in an annual dataset may therefore mean that no new
          estimate was available for that year.
          It does not necessarily mean someone forgot to enter a value.
        </p>

        <p>
          We would need to check the source documentation to establish which
          surveys or estimates produced the values shown here.
          We should not assume that a missing year has the same value as the
          previous year.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-missing-not-random">
          <title>Missing values can tell us something</title>

          <p>
            Missing values can occur for so many different reasons, and knowing
            that reason impacts how to handle it.
            Sometimes entire countries are missing, or gaps are in particular
            years.
            Sometimes they are associated with other characteristics, such as
            income group or child mortality.
            And sometimes an annual value is missing because the underlying data
            is collected only periodically.
          </p>

          <p>
            These patterns matter.
            If we remove every row containing a missing value, we may also
            remove particular countries, years, or groups from our analysis.
            The remaining data may give us a different picture from the one we
            intended to study.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before deciding what to do with missing values, we should first
            investigate where they occur, what might explain them, and how their
            absence could affect our results.
          </p>
        </insight>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-missing-mechanism">
      <title>Why values are missing: the mechanism matters</title>

      <p>
        We can now put names to what we've seen.
        Statisticians describe missing data using three mechanisms, and the
        viral load study had an example of each.
        Here is each one in the context of this dataset.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Missing completely at random (MCAR)</term> means that whether a
        value is missing has nothing to do with the value, or with anything else
        in the data.
        The 219 missing enrolment figures would be MCAR if each country-year had
        simply been lost at random, like the six viral load samples that were
        never collected.
        The values we do have would then still be a fair sample of all 756.
        We'd love to show you a real example of that here, but we can't: every
        gap we've looked at has a pattern.
        Even the 20 missing GDP values are not random, since 12 come from
        Eritrea and 8 from South Sudan.
        Truly MCAR data is rarer than most people hope.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Missing at random (MAR)</term> means that missingness can be
        explained by other variables we <em>have</em> recorded.
        The name is a bit misleading: it doesn't mean the missingness is random,
        only that the pattern is explained by something we can see.
        Primary enrolment is a good candidate.
        It's missing more in recent years and in upper-middle-income countries,
        and we know the year and the income group for every row.
        That means we can compare like with like, for example
        upper-middle-income countries in 2020 against other upper-middle-income
        countries in 2020.
        (We say "candidate" because the higher child mortality we found suggests
        something else might also be going on.
        MAR is always an assumption, not something the data can confirm.)
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Missing not at random (MNAR)</term> means that whether a value is
        missing depends on the value itself, and nothing else in the data can
        fully account for it.
        This is the dangerous one, the sickest patients who were never tested in
        the Kenyan study.
        HIV prevalence might be an example.
        If countries tend not to publish a figure when the epidemic is small, or
        when it's politically sensitive, then the missing values depend on the
        very thing they would have told us.
        The four countries that look wealthier and healthier than the rest are
        consistent with that story, but we can't prove it.
      </p>

      <p>
        And then there are <c>poverty_rate</c> and <c>births_skilled</c>.
        These aren't really any of the three.
        The value never existed because nobody measured it that year.
        The question here is <q>was this ever meant to be there?</q>
      </p>

      <p>
        It's the same conclusion we reached in
        <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, but now we have the words to say
        exactly what's at stake in each case.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-missing-options">
      <title>What to do: three options, each a decision</title>

      <p>
        First, doing nothing is also a choice, and usually not a good one.
        Most R functions return <c>NA</c> if any input is missing:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        mean(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment)
        #&gt; [1] NA
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        This is R refusing to guess.
        The mean of numbers that include some unknowns is itself unknown, and R
        is right to say so.
        But in practice, most of us react by adding <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> and
        moving on, without thinking about what we just decided.
        There are three real options.
      </p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>
          Option 1: Remove the rows ( <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> or listwise deletion)
        </title>

        <p>
          <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> removes the missing values and calculates on
          whatever is left.
          Put simply, you are no longer analysing the dataset you thought you
          were.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
          mean(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment, na.rm = TRUE)
          #&gt; [1] 102.4205
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          This is the mean of 537 country-years, not 756.
          It doesn't estimate the missing values, it just leaves them out
          (unless you check with <c>sum(!is.na(...))</c> yourself).
          And since the missing values were concentrated in recent years and in
          upper-middle-income countries, that 102.4 describes the earlier years
          and the other countries better than it describes everyone.
        </p>

        <p>
          A more aggressive version is listwise deletion, which drops every row
          with a missing value anywhere.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
          sum(complete.cases(africa_indicators))
          #&gt; [1] 22

          nrow(africa_indicators)
          #&gt; [1] 756
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Only 22 of 756 rows are complete!
          That's mostly down to <c>poverty_rate</c> and <c>births_skilled</c>,
          which are missing in most rows.
          Listwise deletion would throw away 97% of the data, including every
          row where the only gap is a variable we don't even need.
          If we only need the other columns, we can choose them first:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
          africa_indicators %&gt;%
            select(-poverty_rate, -births_skilled) %&gt;%
            complete.cases() %&gt;%
            sum()
          #&gt; [1] 491
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          That keeps 491 rows, which is much better, but still a lot!
          <q>Drop the incomplete rows</q> depends entirely on which columns you
          count, so decide what the analysis needs first.
          (And remember that those 491 rows still leave out those four countries
          as they have no HIV figure.)
        </p>

        <remark xml:id="remark-report-n">
          <title>Always report the n</title>

          <p>
            Never report a mean, a standard deviation, or any other summary
            without also saying the <m>n</m> it was calculated from.
            A summary of 756 country-years, 537 country-years, or 22
            country-years is a different claim about a different sample, and a
            table without <m>n</m> makes it impossible to tell them apart.
          </p>
        </remark>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Option 2: Keep the rows and work around the gap</title>

        <p>
          Sometimes the missing values just don't matter for the question.
          If we want life expectancy by income group, the gaps in
          <c>poverty_rate</c> are irrelevant and we can ignore them.
          And when we do summarise a variable with gaps, we can report how many
          are missing, instead of dropping them without saying so:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
          africa_indicators %&gt;%
            group_by(income_group) %&gt;%
            summarise(
              mean_enrolment = mean(primary_enrolment, na.rm = TRUE),
              n              = sum(!is.na(primary_enrolment)),
              n_missing      = sum(is.na(primary_enrolment))
            )
          #&gt; # A tibble: 4 x 4
          #&gt;   income_group        mean_enrolment     n n_missing
          #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;                        &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
          #&gt; 1 Low income                    102.   202        92
          #&gt; 2 Lower middle income           103.   255        81
          #&gt; 3 Upper middle income           101.    66        46
          #&gt; 4 High income                   115.    14         0
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          This table is honest about what it's built on.
          Nobody reading it can miss that for upper-middle-income countries, 46
          out of 112 values are absent, so that 101 is based on just 66.
          Whether you'd trust it for a report depends on what the report is for.
          Also, because we compare within each group, this is the "like with
          like" approach we described for MAR.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Option 3: Fill in an estimate (imputation)</title>

        <p>
          <term>Imputation</term> fills in each missing value with an estimate,
          often the mean of the variable, so no rows need to be dropped.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
          enrolment_imputed &lt;- ifelse(
            is.na(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment),
            mean(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment, na.rm = TRUE),
            africa_indicators$primary_enrolment
          )

          sd(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment, na.rm = TRUE)
          #&gt; [1] 19.92553

          sd(enrolment_imputed)
          #&gt; [1] 16.78877
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          This runs without complaint and gives a column with no gaps.
          But we've replaced 219 honestly missing values with 219 identical
          copies of 102.4.
          The mean doesn't change, but the standard deviation drops from 19.9 to
          16.8, because we've added lots of values sitting exactly in the
          middle.
          The data now looks more consistent than it really is.
        </p>

        <p>
          For enrolment, where we think the missingness is roughly MAR, that's
          an acceptable shortcut for a quick look.
          For HIV prevalence, it's much worse.
          The mean of the 700 reported values is 4.26 (the median is just 1.6),
          so we'd be giving Mauritius, Seychelles, Equatorial Guinea and São
          Tomé and Príncipe a prevalence of 4.26 each.
          If the reason those countries have no figure is that their epidemics
          are small, we've replaced four honest unknowns with four confidently
          wrong numbers, and hidden the problem rather than acknowledging it.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <insight xml:id="insight-missing-golden-rule">
        <title>The golden rule: be explicit</title>

        <p>
          Whichever approach you choose, the most important thing is to say what
          you did and why.
          <q>We excluded 219 country-years with no primary enrolment figure.
          These were more common after 2018 and in upper-middle-income
          countries, so our estimate may describe earlier years and poorer
          countries better than recent years and richer ones.</q>
          That takes thirty seconds to write, and it's the difference between an
          analysis that can be checked and one that can't.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        The table below summarises the three options and when each is most
        appropriate:
      </p>

      <tabular halign="left">
        <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="40%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="40%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Approach</cell>
          <cell>When it is reasonable</cell>
          <cell>When to be cautious</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Remove the rows</cell>
          <cell>
            Few missing values; missingness appears roughly random; the removed
            rows are not needed for the specific analysis
          </cell>
          <cell>
            Missingness is related to the outcome or a key grouping variable;
            many values missing
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Keep and work around</cell>
          <cell>
            The variable with missing values is not needed for the specific
            analysis; you want to report the missing group separately
          </cell>
          <cell>
            The missing values are in the outcome variable or a required
            predictor
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Impute</cell>
          <cell>
            Many missing values that cannot be dropped; other variables are good
            predictors of the missing value
          </cell>
          <cell>
            Always: imputation introduces assumptions that must be stated and
            justified
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <insight xml:id="insight-missing-decision">
        <title>
          Every missing value needs a decision, and the decision should be
          stated
        </title>

        <p>
          There's no option that avoids making a choice about missing values.
          Leaving them as <c>NA</c> is a choice.
          Using <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> is a choice.
          Listwise deletion is a choice.
          Imputation is a choice.
          What separates good practice from bad is in how you make the choice -
          make it deliberately, say why, and acknowledge how much your
          conclusions depend on it.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-handling-missing-values">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-wald-survivorship">
      <title>The Bombers That Didn't Come Back</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          During the Second World War, the US military looked at bombers coming
          back from missions over Germany, and recorded where each one had been
          hit.
          The damage wasn't evenly spread.
          There was far more of it on the wings and fuselage than on the
          engines.
          The obvious recommendation followed straight away: reinforce the wings
          and fuselage, since that's clearly where the bullets go.
        </p>

        <p>
          The mathematician Abraham Wald was working with a team the US military
          had put together to bring statistics to exactly this kind of problem,
          and he disagreed.
          The damage counts were almost certainly accurate.
          His objection was about which planes had been counted at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          Every bomber in that dataset had survived its mission and made it
          home.
          A bomber that was hit badly enough in the engine didn't come back to
          be inspected, photographed, and added to the tally.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Whose damage is missing from this dataset, and why?
            Think about what has to be true of a bomber for it to end up
            recorded at all.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            The planes missing from the dataset are the ones that were shot
            down, or damaged so badly that they never made it back to base.
            A plane only gets inspected and added to the tally if it survives
            the mission and lands, so the dataset only ever tells you about
            <q>damage a plane can survive</q>, and never about
            <q>damage that
            brings a plane down</q>.
            Whether a plane appears in the data at all depends directly on how
            badly it was hit, and where.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-b">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The engines show very little damage in the data.
            Give two different explanations for that pattern, one where the
            engines really were rarely hit, and one where they were hit just as
            often as anywhere else.
            What would have to be true of the missing planes for each
            explanation to be correct?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            <term>Explanation one: engines really were rarely hit.</term> This
            would be true if enemy fire, for whatever reason, tended to miss the
            engines and land on the wings and fuselage instead, perhaps because
            of the plane's shape, flight path, or the positioning of gunners.
            Under this explanation, the missing planes (the ones that didn't
            come back) would show roughly the same pattern as the surviving
            planes: mostly wing and fuselage hits, occasionally an engine hit.
          </p>

          <p>
            <term>Explanation two: engines were hit just as often, but rarely
      survived.</term>
            This would be true if hits were spread fairly evenly across the
            whole aircraft, but a hit to the engine was usually fatal to the
            mission, while a hit to the wing or fuselage usually wasn't.
            Under this explanation, the missing planes would show a very
            different pattern from the surviving planes: heavily concentrated on
            the engines, which is exactly why none of them are in the data to
            show it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The dataset alone can't distinguish between these two explanations,
            because it only contains the survivors either way.
            Wald's contribution was recognising that the second explanation was
            far more plausible on engineering grounds, and building a method
            that assumed it.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-c">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose you simply excluded any plane you had no data for, in this
            case, this is every plane that never returned, and analysed only the
            bombers that came back.
            Would this be safe?
            Compare this to a case where a few data logs were lost to a filing
            error unrelated to how damaged the plane was.
            Why does one gap sink the analysis and the other doesn't?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            It would not be safe, and it's already exactly what happened, since
            the data was never gathered on the planes that didn't return in the
            first place.
            The problem is that whether a plane is missing is tied directly to
            how much damage it took, and specifically where.
            Missing planes aren't a random subset of all planes.
            They're the worst-damaged ones, concentrated in one part of the
            aircraft.
            Removing them (or, as happened, never having them) removes exactly
            the evidence you'd need to see the true pattern.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that to a handful of paperwork logs lost in a filing error.
            If which logs get lost has nothing to do with where the plane was
            hit, some records for lightly-damaged planes and some for
            heavily-damaged planes will disappear at roughly the same rate.
            The planes you can still see remain a fair picture of all planes,
            just a slightly smaller one.
            One kind of gap changes what conclusion you'd draw.
            The other just costs you a bit of precision.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-d">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Wald's actual recommendation was to reinforce the sections that were
            <em>least</em> represented in the damage data, not the most.
            Using your answer to <xref ref="ex-wald-a"/>, explain why that's the
            opposite conclusion from the obvious one, and why it's the correct
            one.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            The obvious recommendation treats heavy damage in the data as a sign
            of a weak spot:
            <q>we see a lot of wing damage, so wings must be
            vulnerable, so let's reinforce them.</q>
            But because the data only contains survivors, lots of visible damage
            to a section is actually evidence that the section can take a hit
            and keep flying.
            It's a sign of strength.
          </p>

          <p>
            A section that's barely damaged in the data, like the engines, isn't
            necessarily a section that rarely gets hit.
            Given that hits are spread fairly evenly over the whole airframe,
            its absence from the data is far better explained by planes hit
            there not surviving to be counted.
            So the section with the least visible damage is the one hiding the
            planes you never got to see, which is exactly the section that most
            needs reinforcing.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-e">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Could you fix this problem by simply gathering more data, for
            example, inspecting even more returning bombers?
            Why or why not?
            What kind of missing data can be fixed by collecting more of the
            same kind of records, and what kind can't?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            No.
            Inspecting more returning bombers only gives you more survivors, and
            survivors are exactly the group that's already overrepresented.
            No matter how many more of them you inspect, you'll never see the
            damage pattern on a plane that didn't make it back, because those
            planes are permanently absent from the pool you're sampling from.
            More data of the same kind just makes you more confident in the
            wrong picture.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is the key difference from the filing-error case in
            <xref ref="ex-wald-c"/>.
            If your gap is caused by something unrelated to the thing you're
            measuring, like a lost log, collecting more records genuinely helps,
            because the new records aren't systematically excluding a whole
            category of outcome.
            But if the gap exists <em>because</em> of the thing you're
            measuring, like a plane not surviving because of where it was hit,
            no amount of additional data collected the same way will ever
            recover it.
            You need a different source of information entirely (in Wald's case,
            the logic of the missingness itself) rather than more of what you
            already have.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-challenger-missing">
      <title>The Launches That Weren't on the Chart</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="ex-challenger"/> we looked at the night before the
          Challenger launch in January 1986.
          Engineers at Morton Thiokol, the company that built the rocket
          boosters, argued the launch should be delayed because the O-ring seals
          could fail in the cold.
          NASA went ahead, and all seven crew members were killed.
        </p>

        <p>
          Edward Tufte's Challenger graph is a famous case study in data
          visualisation.
          It shows how a scatter plot of O-ring damage against launch
          temperature could have made the dangers of the launch really clear.
          In his book <em>Visual Explanations</em> (1997), Tufte argued that the
          disaster was partly a failure of data communication: the engineers had
          the evidence, but it wasn't shown in a way that let anyone see the
          pattern.
        </p>

        <p>
          Tufte's argument, and our exercise, focused on how the data was shown.
          But there's another problem with the charts the engineers used, and
          it's the same kind of problem as Wald's bombers.
          When they looked at whether temperature mattered, they only included
          launches where O-ring damage had been found.
          The launches with no damage at all weren't there.
        </p>

        <p>
          We use the same <c>orings</c> data from the <c>DAAG</c> package.
          Each row is one launch before Challenger, <c>Temperature</c> is the
          launch temperature in degrees Fahrenheit, and <c>Total</c> is the
          number of O-rings showing damage.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
            library(DAAG)
            library(dplyr)
            library(ggplot2)
          </code>
        </program>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Filter <c>orings</c> down to the launches where <c>Total &gt; 0</c>,
            which is what the engineers' charts showed.
            How many launches are in the full data, and how many are left after
            filtering?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <program language="r">
            <code>
              orings_damaged &lt;- orings %&gt;%
                filter(Total &gt; 0)

              nrow(orings)
              nrow(orings_damaged)
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            Most of the launches disappear.
            Only the minority with some damage are left, so any chart built from
            <c>orings_damaged</c> is describing a much smaller, and very
            particular, group of launches.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-b">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Plot <c>Temperature</c> against <c>Total</c> twice: once for all
            launches, and once for the damaged launches only.
            Add the forecast temperature for the Challenger launch (26 to 29°F)
            as a shaded region, as you did in <xref ref="ex-challenger-b"/>.
            How does the relationship between temperature and damage change when
            the zero-damage launches are left out?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <program language="r">
            <code>
              ggplot(orings_damaged, aes(x = Temperature, y = Total)) +
                geom_point() +
                xlim(25, 85) +
                ylim(0, 6) +
                annotate("rect",
                         xmin = 26, xmax = 29,
                         ymin = 0,  ymax = 6,
                         alpha = 0.3, fill = "red") +
                annotate("text",
                         x = 33, y = 3,
                         label = "Forecast\ntemperature\n26 to 29°F",
                         hjust = 0) +
                labs(x = "Temperature (°F)",
                     y = "Total O-ring damage",
                     title = "O-ring damage by launch temperature",
                     subtitle = "Launches with damage only: zero-damage launches excluded") +
                theme_bw()
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            For the full data, swap <c>orings_damaged</c> for <c>orings</c> and
            change the subtitle.
          </p>

          <p>
            With every launch included, the pattern is clear: the cold launches
            all had damage, and nearly all the damage-free launches happened at
            warmer temperatures.
            Take the zero-damage launches away and that pattern really fades.
            What's left is damage scattered across a range of temperatures, and
            it looks like damage can happen whether it's cold or warm.
            The launches that were left out were the ones that showed warm
            launches tend to go fine, and that was the evidence that cold was
            the problem.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-c">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What decided whether a launch ended up on the chart?
            Were the launches that got left out just a random handful, or was
            there something about them that made them go missing?
            How does that compare to Wald's bombers?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            It wasn't random at all.
            Whether a launch was included depended on its value of <c>Total</c>:
            launches with zero damage were dropped, and launches with some
            damage were kept.
            So the launches went missing because of the very thing we were
            trying to measure.
            That's the same structure as the bombers, where whether a plane was
            in the data depended on how badly it had been hit.
            And just like the bombers, it means the data that's left gives us a
            distorted picture, not just a smaller one.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-d">
        <statement>
          <p>
            In <xref ref="ex-wald-e"/> we saw that no amount of extra data
            collected the same way could fix Wald's problem.
            Is the Challenger case the same?
            What would it have taken to fix it?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            No, and this is what makes the Challenger case so hard to look at.
            Wald's missing planes were lost over enemy territory, and nobody
            could ever inspect them.
            The zero-damage launches were never missing from the records.
            The engineers had that data; it just didn't make it onto the chart.
            The fix didn't need new data or a clever model of the missingness.
            It needed someone to ask
            <q>which launches are we not looking at, and why?</q>
          </p>

          <p>
            Missing data isn't only something that happens to us during data
            collection.
            We can also create it ourselves, with a filter or a decision about
            what's <q>relevant</q>, and it can do just as much damage.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <conclusion>
        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Tufte, E.R.
          (1997).
          <em>Visual Explanations: Images and Quantities, Evidence and
          Narrative.</em>
          Graphics Press.
          Data from the <c>DAAG</c> R package.
          Case study adapted from Helveston, J.P.
          (2019), George Washington University,
          <url href="https://p4a.seas.gwu.edu/2019-Fall/challengerExplosion.html"/>,
          CC BY 4.0.
        </p>
      </conclusion>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-imputation-risk">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What would happen to the mean and the standard deviation of
          <c>body_mass_g</c> if you used <c>mass_imputed</c>, instead of the
          original column with <c>na.rm = TRUE</c>.
          Which one changes, which one doesn't, and why does that matter for
          anyone reading a report built on this column?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: missing values -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-missing-values">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The Kenya Road Accidents database contains missing values in several
          columns.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Write R code to count the number of missing values in each column.
              Which column has the most missing values?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The <c>AGE</c> column has both true missing values and coded
              values like <c>A</c> and <c>J</c>.
              After converting <c>AGE</c> to numeric, how many <c>NA</c> s
              result?
              Are all of these genuinely unknown ages, or are some of them
              something else?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Explain in your own words the difference between a value that is
              missing and a value that is unknown.
              Does that distinction matter for this dataset?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>